"Qilin": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Tuhui_zongyi_%28Principles_of_Painting%29_Qilin.jpeg","title":"The qilin in a Ming painting manual","desc":"A woodblock plate from the <i>Tuhui zongyi</i>, the painting manual edited by Yang Erzeng and printed in 1607; the copy reproduced is in the Philadelphia Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuhui_zongyi_(Principles_of_Painting)_Qilin.jpeg","alt":"A woodblock plate in black line on aged paper: a scaled, cloven-hoofed beast with a bearded dragon-like head, a pair of horns swept back over its shoulders and a bushy flame-shaped tail, bounding through grass beneath a pine, with its name in two Chinese characters at the upper right."},
"Quaternary": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/The_Quaternary_at_the_NMNH%2C_Bulgaria.jpg/1920px-The_Quaternary_at_the_NMNH%2C_Bulgaria.jpg","title":"Quaternary","desc":"The Quaternary at the National Museum of Natural History, Bulgaria. Motekov, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Quaternary_at_the_NMNH,_Bulgaria.jpg","alt":"The Quaternary at the NMNH, Bulgaria"},
"Quaternary_extinction_event": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg/1920px-Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg","title":"Quaternary extinction event","desc":"Woolly mammoths were driven to extinction by climate change and human impacts. The image depicts a late Pleistocene landscape in northern Spain with woolly mammoths (Mammuthus. primigenius ), equids , a woolly rhinoceros ( Coelodonta antiquitatis ), and European cave lions ( Panthera leo spelaea )… Mauricio Antón, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg","alt":"Ice age fauna of northern Spain - Mauricio Antón"},
"Queen_Mother_of_the_West": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West%2C_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg/1280px-Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West%2C_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg","title":"Peach Festival of the Queen Mother of the West","desc":"A handscroll in ink and colour on silk, Ming or Qing dynasty, 17th or 18th century, by an anonymous painter once credited to Fang Chunnian. Freer Gallery of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West,_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg","alt":"A blue-and-green landscape painting: a terraced palace of tiled halls and pavilions stands on a stone platform among pines, small figures on its balconies and crossing a bridge below, with green and blue peaks and banked gold cloud behind and an orchard at the right."},
"Radiocarbon_dating": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Radiocarbon_calibration_error_and_measurement_error.png","title":"Radiocarbon dating","desc":"Partial graph of INTCAL13 data, showing variation in calibration curve results. The source for the underlying data is here, via the \"intcal13.14c\" link. Mike Christie, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radiocarbon_calibration_error_and_measurement_error.png","alt":"Radiocarbon calibration error and measurement error"},
"Raymond_Dart": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/Raymond_Dart-_Smithsonian_Institution_Archives_-_SIA-SIA2008-0845_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Raymond Dart","desc":"Australian-born anatomist and paleoanthropologist Raymond Arthur Dart (1893-1988), a professor and dean at the University of Witwatersrand, Johannesburg, South Africa, 1925-1943, was best known for his 1924 discovery of fossil remains of Australopithecus africanus Full description. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raymond_Dart-_Smithsonian_Institution_Archives_-_SIA-SIA2008-0845_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Raymond Dart- Smithsonian Institution Archives - SIA-SIA2008-0845"},
"Recent_African_origin_of_modern_humans": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_%282023%29.png/1920px-Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_%282023%29.png","title":"Recent African origin of modern humans","desc":"This map shows the migration routes of modern humans within and Out of Africa, based on archaeogenetic data and environmental factors. The map was designed and created by me, Jayasinghe Sevan, in 2023. Jayasinghe23, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_(2023).png","alt":"Migration routes of modern humans (2023)"},
