"Temple_of_Artemis,_Corfu": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg/1920px-West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg","title":"West pediment of the Temple of Artemis at Corfu","desc":"The reconstructed west pediment, with the winged Gorgon at the centre, a great cat on either side and small figures in the corners. Dr.K., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Dr.K., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg","alt":"A wide shallow stone gable. A crouching winged figure with a grimacing face fills the centre, a long cat lies on each side of her, and tiny figures stand in the narrow corners."},
"Temple_of_Hera,_Olympia": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Curtius_Olympia_3_t_02.jpg","title":"The Heraion of Olympia under excavation","desc":"The Heraion seen from the south-east during the German excavations, with workmen standing at points of the plan. Photograph by Gebr. Romaidis of Patras, published in Ernst Curtius, ed., Die Ausgrabungen zu Olympia, vol. 3 (Berlin, 1877-78). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Gebr. Romaidis, Patras, in Ernst Curtius, ed., Die Ausgrabungen zu Olympia, vol. 3 (Berlin, 1877-78), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curtius_Olympia_3_t_02.jpg","alt":"A temple reduced to its lowest courses and stumps of columns, with drums and capitals lying on the ground and men standing among them on a hillside."},
"Ten_Kings_of_Hell": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/China%2C_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-China%2C_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif.jpg","title":"A king of hell in his hall","desc":"Leaf 29 of a Chinese album of Daoist and Buddhist themes, painted in the 1200s and belonging to the album's series on the Ten Kings of Hells; the Cleveland Museum of Art, which holds it, says the king's identity is unclear. Made available under CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China,_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif","alt":"An ink outline drawing on tan paper, with no colour but a red seal at the foot. A bearded official in a winged cap sits on a throne at the head of a flight of steps, holding a narrow plaque across his chest; a hanging scroll beside him shows a plan of walled buildings. Attendants stand below the dais with trays and staves, an armoured demon waits at the left and a beaked demon at the right, and two old men in long robes bow with their hands clasped at the foot of the steps."},
"Terramare_culture": {"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi%2C_1877.jpg/1920px-Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi%2C_1877.jpg","title":"Terramare culture","desc":"Fotografia tratta da L. Pigorini, Terramara dell’età del Bronzo situata in Castione dei Marchesi (territorio parmigiano), in “Atti Reale Accademia dei Lincei”, s. III, VIII, 1882-83. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi,_1877.jpg","alt":"Scavi alla Terramara di Castione Marchesi, 1877"},
